% ABSTRACT
\sectiontitle{Project Abstract (2 pts)}

\vspace{0.05cm}

\instruction{

}

\vspace{0.12cm}

\answerbox{8.00cm}{PrioriTask is a C++ desktop and command-line application that uses SQLite to help students manage their daily schedules, especially when they have more tasks to complete than the available hours. What makes PrioriTask different from regular to-do list applications is its **task compression feature**. Instead of simply skipping tasks when there isn't enough time, PrioriTask adjusts the duration of each task according to its priority and flexibility.

Each task has two durations: the ideal time needed to complete it comfortably and the minimum time required to get it done. For example, a 60-minute gym workout can be reduced to 30 minutes on a busy day, while an important assignment is given more time based on its priority. This helps students make the best use of their available time without completely missing out on important tasks.

\vspace{4pt}
In Phase-III the project grew from a scheduling engine into a complete application. When even the minimums
do not fit, a task-dropping module moves the least valuable optional tasks to another day and never drops a
mandatory task. A rule-based advisor answers ``What should I do now?'' using deadlines, priority and the
user's current energy level. Fixed commitments such as college or office hours are subtracted from the
available time, and during these hours the application stays quiet unless the user declares a free slot. The
application has two interfaces, an 11-option command-line menu and a four-tab desktop window, which share
one engine and one database. All data structures and algorithms, including the dynamic array, binary heap
and merge sort, are written by the team instead of being taken from the C++ STL.}

